  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A chicken lost its head and lived eighteen more months. The 10th of September, 1945. Fruita, Colorado. Farmer Lloyd Olsen swung the axe.",
        "visual_query": "colorado farm chicken coop",
        "visual_fallback": "farmyard hens"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Mike got up. He tried to preen. He tried to crow. Olsen fed him milk and water through an eyedropper, plus corn and worms.",
        "visual_query": "rural farmyard hens feeding",
        "visual_fallback": "chickens feeding"
      },
      {
        "narration": "This was no twitch. Mike toured sideshows at 25 cents a look, and at his peak Olsen cleared 4,500 dollars a month.",
        "visual_query": "1940s american sideshow carnival",
        "visual_fallback": "old carnival"
      },
      {
        "narration": "So how? The blade missed the jugular and left one ear and most of the brain stem intact. Breathing, heart rate, reflexes \u2014 the brain stem runs all of it.",
        "visual_query": "vintage anatomy medical illustration",
        "visual_fallback": "medical diagram"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Mike died in March 1947, choking at a motel after the Olsens left his feeding syringes behind. Fruita still holds him a festival every May.",
        "visual_query": "small colorado town street",
        "visual_fallback": "small town"
      }
    ],
